ed spanning subgraphs.*\n\nBy repeatedly applying Theorem 1, Hörsch [5] obtained the bound \\(c_k \\le 16 \\log k\\). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe 2-balanced double tree decomposition conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/354571/the-2-balanced-double-tree-decomposition-conjecture)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # The 2-balanced double tree decomposition conjecture ... A double tree is a graph formed by taking two trees on the same vertex set, with the two edge sets regarded as distinct even when they join the same pair of vertices.\n\n# The 2-balanced double tree decomposition conjecture\n\nAbout 4 years old · traced to 2022\n\nA double tree is a graph formed by taking two trees on the same vertex set, with the two edge sets regarded as distinct even when they join the same pair of vertices. A double tree decomposition partitions the edges of a double tree into two trees. Such a decomposition is $k$k-balanced if, at every vertex, the absolute difference between its degrees in the two trees is at most $k$k.\n\n2-balanced double tree decomposition conjecture. Any double tree has a $2$2-balanced double tree decomposition.\n\nThe paper proves that every double tree has a decomposition that is $4$4-balanced, improving the previously known bound of five. Examples are known that admit a $2$2-balanced decomposition but no $1$1-balanced one, so the conjectured bound would be best possible; the conjecture remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nFreddie Illingworth, Emil Powierski, Alex Scott and Youri Tamitegama, “Balancing connected colourings of graphs”, arXiv:2205.04984 (2022).\n\ngraph-colouringsgraph-theorytree-decompositions\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Po